<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Compagnie des Îles de l'Amérique</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1635">1635</a>–<a href="/years/1651">1651</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Company of the American Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Compagnie des Îles de l'Amérique was a French chartered company founded in 1635 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> to colonise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. It succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compagnie-de-saint-christophe">Compagnie de Saint-Christophe</a> and sponsored the settlement of Guadeloupe and Martinique in 1635. Unable to make a profit, it sold its islands in 1649–1651 to private proprietors, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, who bought Saint Christopher and neighbouring islands.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1635 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a>, a shareholder in the struggling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compagnie-de-saint-christophe">Compagnie de Saint-Christophe</a>, charged François Fouquet, head of a small group of his councillors, with reviving it, and Fouquet renamed it. The company took over the administration of the French part of Saint-Christophe, then the only French settlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, with a mandate to develop the other islands of the Antilles and convert their inhabitants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 15 September 1635 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-belain-desnambuc">Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc</a>, French governor of St. Kitts, landed at St. Pierre in Martinique with 150 settlers after being driven off St. Kitts by the English, claimed the island for Louis XIII and the company, and founded Fort Saint-Pierre under governor Jean Dupont. After d'Esnambuc's death in 1636 his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-dyel-du-parquet">Jacques Dyel du Parquet</a> became governor in 1637; he colonised Martinique, founded the first settlement in Saint Lucia in 1643 and led the expedition that settled Grenada in 1649.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-martin-island">Saint Martin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-barthelemy">Saint Barthélemy</a> followed in 1648 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">St. Croix</a> in 1650. In 1642 the company's charter was extended for twenty years: the king would name its governor general and the company the governors of the islands. On Saint Christopher, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phillippe-de-longvilliers-de-poincy">Phillippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</a>, Bailiff Grand Cross of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights of Malta</a>, governed from 1639 until his death in 1660, first for the company and then for the order itself.</p><p>By the late 1640s Mazarin took little interest in colonial affairs and the company languished. It dissolved itself in 1651, having sold its rights: the du Parquet family bought Martinique, Grenada and Saint Lucia for 60,000 livres, the sieur d'Houël Guadeloupe, Marie-Galante, La Desirade and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintes">Saintes</a>, and the Knights of Malta Saint-Christophe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-barthelemy">Saint Barthélemy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-martin-island">Saint Martin</a>. In 1665 the Knights sold their islands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">Compagnie des Indes occidentales</a>, formed in 1664.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Company of the American Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles Liénard de l'Olive and Jean du Plessis d'Ossonville landed in 1635 for the <b>Compagnie des Îles d'Amérique</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-guadeloupe">Colony of Guadeloupe</a></li><li><blockquote>Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc landed at Saint-Pierre in 1635 and claimed the island for the <b>Compagnie des Îles d'Amérique</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-martinique">Colony of Martinique</a></li><li><blockquote>Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy, a Knight of Malta and governor-general of the French Antilles, persuaded the order to buy the French shares of Saint Kitts, Saint Martin, Saint-Barthélemy and Saint Croix from the bankrupt <b>Compagnie des Îles d'Amérique</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hospitaller-caribbean-colonies">Hospitaller Caribbean colonies</a></li></ul></section>
